WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.511
Every designed place distributes something.

2
00:00:02.511 --> 00:00:05.021
A sidewalk distributes safety and mobility.

3
00:00:05.021 --> 00:00:08.524
A park distributes shade, recreation, and public investment.

4
00:00:08.524 --> 00:00:11.502
A zoning map distributes permission and constraint.

5
00:00:11.502 --> 00:00:16.231
A meeting notice distributes information and the practical chance to participate.

6
00:00:16.231 --> 00:00:23.062
Design equity begins by asking who receives the benefits, who carries the burdens, and who has power over the choice.

7
00:00:23.062 --> 00:00:36.257
This episode adapts chapter one of Kristine Miller's Introduction to Design Equity, published by University of Minnesota Libraries Publishing and available through Engineering LibreTexts under Creative Commons Attribution 4.0.

8
00:00:36.257 --> 00:00:41.862
The narration condenses and reorganizes Miller's ideas and adds a separate Torrance application.

9
00:00:41.862 --> 00:00:47.759
Third-party readings, images, quotations, statistics, and linked media from the chapter are excluded.

10
00:00:47.759 --> 00:00:50.739
Equality and equity are not interchangeable tests.

11
00:00:50.739 --> 00:00:56.460
Giving every neighborhood the same object can look equal while leaving unequal access untouched.

12
00:00:56.460 --> 00:01:02.420
An equity analysis starts with conditions and asks what each community needs to reach a fair result.

13
00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:05.400
It also asks how present conditions were produced.

14
00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:12.552
A missing sidewalk, distant grocery store, dangerous crossing, or inaccessible meeting is not simply an isolated defect.

15
00:01:12.552 --> 00:01:14.996
It can be part of a pattern of decisions.

16
00:01:14.996 --> 00:01:17.558
Design is larger than professional styling.

17
00:01:17.558 --> 00:01:22.863
Policies, services, forms, schedules, budgets, buildings, and streets all shape behavior.

18
00:01:22.863 --> 00:01:25.843
Their effects can differ from their stated intent.

19
00:01:25.843 --> 00:01:30.909
A decision-maker therefore needs an impact question as well as an intention question.

20
00:01:30.909 --> 00:01:31.922
Who can use this?

21
00:01:31.922 --> 00:01:32.696
At what cost?

22
00:01:32.696 --> 00:01:33.471
At what time?

23
00:01:33.471 --> 00:01:34.425
With what risks?

24
00:01:34.425 --> 00:01:36.034
Who cannot use it, and why?

25
00:01:36.034 --> 00:01:37.876
Use a four-column equity ledger.

26
00:01:37.876 --> 00:01:40.122
In the first column, name the decision.

27
00:01:40.122 --> 00:01:43.001
In the second, list expected benefits and burdens.

28
00:01:43.001 --> 00:01:47.261
In the third, identify groups and places likely to experience each effect.

29
00:01:47.261 --> 00:01:52.098
In the fourth, record who helped define the problem and who can change the proposal.

30
00:01:52.098 --> 00:01:55.092
Do not treat demographic categories as explanations.

31
00:01:55.092 --> 00:01:59.928
Pair quantitative patterns with direct community knowledge and document uncertainty.

32
00:01:59.928 --> 00:02:01.425
Equity is also procedural.

33
00:02:01.425 --> 00:02:08.219
Inviting comments after the major choices are fixed is different from sharing power while alternatives are still open.

34
00:02:08.219 --> 00:02:11.789
Track which ideas changed the project, which did not, and why.

35
00:02:11.789 --> 00:02:14.610
That decision trail makes participation testable.

36
00:02:14.610 --> 00:02:16.303
Torrance application segment.

37
00:02:16.303 --> 00:02:20.391
This segment is original independent study, not part of Miller's book.

38
00:02:20.391 --> 00:02:26.755
The cited local source is the City of Torrance General Plan page, checked October seventh, twenty twenty-six.

39
00:02:26.755 --> 00:02:34.930
The page describes the General Plan as the foundation for land-use decisions and says the City is updating its Land Use and Safety Elements.

40
00:02:34.930 --> 00:02:40.594
For a hypothetical element workshop, apply the equity ledger before selecting a preferred policy.

41
00:02:40.594 --> 00:02:47.308
Compare who gains access to housing, jobs, public facilities, cleaner air, and safer travel under each alternative.

42
00:02:47.308 --> 00:02:52.622
Identify burdens such as displacement pressure, noise, traffic exposure, or reduced access.

43
00:02:52.622 --> 00:02:55.600
Then publish the assumptions and invite correction.

44
00:02:55.600 --> 00:02:59.979
This exercise does not judge Torrance's legal compliance or adopted policy.

45
00:02:59.979 --> 00:03:01.988
The practical lesson is simple.

46
00:03:01.988 --> 00:03:05.358
Equity is not a decorative value added after design.

47
00:03:05.358 --> 00:03:11.968
It is a method for defining the problem, distributing power, comparing effects, and checking outcomes.

48
00:03:11.968 --> 00:03:16.246
This adaptation credits Kristine Miller and is licensed CC BY 4.0.

49
00:03:16.246 --> 00:03:23.115
Changes include condensation, paraphrase, reorganization, and the separately sourced Torrance application.

50
00:03:23.115 --> 00:03:24.800
No endorsement is implied.
